<article class="env-proposition" id="0GIJ">
  <p><a class="environment-identifier" href="/tag/0GIJ">Proposition <span data-tag="0GIJ">45.11.6</span>.</a> Let $k$ be a field. Let $F$ be a field of characteristic $0$. A Weil cohomology theory is the same thing as a $\mathbf{Q}$-linear symmetric monoidal functor </p>
  <div class="equation">
  \[  G : M_ k \longrightarrow \text{graded }F\text{-vector spaces}  \]
</div>
  <p> such that </p>
  <p><ol>
  <li><p>
$G(\mathbf{1}(1))$ is nonzero only in degree $-2$, and 
</p>
</li><li><p>
for every smooth projective scheme $X$ over $k$ with $k' = \Gamma (X, \mathcal{O}_ X)$ the homomorphism $G(h(\mathop{\mathrm{Spec}}(k'))) \to G(h(X))$ of graded $F$-vector spaces is an isomorphism in degree $0$. 
</p>
</li>
</ol></p>
</article><article class="env-proof">
    <p>
      <strong>Proof.</strong>
      Immediate consequence of Proposition <a href="/tag/0FHM" data-tag="0FHM">45.9.12</a> and Definition <a href="/tag/0FI2" data-tag="0FI2">45.11.4</a>. Of course we could replace (2) by the condition that $G(h(X)) \to \bigoplus G(h(x_ i))$ is injective in degree $0$ for some choice of closed points $x_1, \ldots , x_ r \in X$ whose residue fields are separable over $k$. 
      <span class="qed">$\square$</span>
    </p>
</article>